        -: 1895:    // NB: we must have DoSomething() function when Something() is an overloaded
        -: 1896:    //     method: indeed, we can't just have "virtual Something()" in case when
        -: 1897:    //     the function is overloaded because then we'd have to make virtual all
        -: 1898:    //     the variants (otherwise only the virtual function may be called on a
        -: 1899:    //     pointer to derived class according to C++ rules) which is, in
        -: 1900:    //     general, absolutely not needed. So instead we implement all
        -: 1901:    //     overloaded Something()s in terms of DoSomething() which will be the
        -: 1902:    //     only one to be virtual.
